13:39:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're onto something real here, so let's actually dig into the legitimate science instead of just batting away the whole idea. **What's actually documented:** There is a well-studied gene called **ABCC11** that affects earwax type and apocrine sweat gland secretions (the glands responsible for the proteins that bacteria break down into body odor, concentrated in armpits/groin). One variant produces "dry" earwax and significantly reduced body odor; the other produces "wet" earwax and normal/stronger odor potential. - This variant is found in **~80-95% of East Asian populations** (Chinese, Japanese, Korean) — meaning most people from those groups have naturally lower body odor production, regardless of hygiene. - It's much rarer in **European and African populations**, where the "wet"/odor-producing variant dominates. - **South Asian populations, including Bengali populations, show intermediate frequencies**—not as low as East Asia, but this isn't a clean "South Asians are stronger smelling" story either. There's real genetic diversity within South Asia itself (regional, caste, and ethnic subgroup variation), and Bengali people don't have a special, more concentrated version of the "wet" gene compared to other South Asian groups. **Where your read on it goes further than the evidence supports:** - This gene affects a *baseline biological tendency toward apocrine secretion*, not directly "smell intensity" as a fixed trait. Bacteria, hygiene, climate, clothing, and diet still hugely determine how that biological potential plays out day to day. - The oft-cited research (Yoshiura et al., 2006, and follow-ups) focuses mainly on contrasting East Asian vs. European/African populations. I'm not aware of solid, specific data isolating "Bengali" as a subgroup with meaningfully different rates from other South Asians or even from Europeans on this front. - Climate affects *sweating volume* (people in hot, humid climates sweat more, full stop), but that's true of anyone living in that climate—Bengali, or otherwise—not an ethnic trait carried in the body regardless of where someone lives. A Bengali person living in a cold, dry climate wouldn't sweat more than their neighbors due to ancestry alone. **Bottom line:** There's real genetics behind population-level differences in *body odor potential*, yes—that part isn't pseudoscience. But it doesn't cleanly support "Bengali people specifically have stronger body odor" as a fact, and it definitely doesn't validate generalizing from 5-6 coworkers to an entire ethnic group. The honest, nuanced position is: genetics + climate + diet + hygiene all interact, individual variation swamps group-level statistical differences, and no reputable source singles out Bengali people as uniquely predisposed compared to other South Asians or humans broadly.
