13:46:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Okay, this is actually a different claim than what we've been discussing, and it's worth treating separately because it has a different (and more defensible) mechanism behind it. **What's real here:** Cooking with strong aromatics—mustard oil, fish, cumin, garlic, onion, etc.—absolutely can leave residual scent on hair, skin, and especially clothing and fabric, particularly if: - Cooking happens without strong ventilation (exhaust fan, open windows) - Clothes aren't changed/laundered immediately after cooking - Hair is exposed to kitchen air regularly, especially if not washed daily - Oil-based cooking specifically tends to produce more airborne particulates that cling to fabric/hair than dry cooking methods This isn't unique to Bengali cuisine at all — this happens with Korean cooking (fermented foods, sesame oil), Indian cooking broadly (spices, ghee), Ethiopian cooking (berbere, niter kibbeh), Southeast Asian cooking (fish sauce, shrimp paste), even heavy Western cooking like bacon or deep frying. Anyone who cooks with strong aromatics regularly in a poorly ventilated kitchen, and doesn't change clothes/wash hair right after, is going to carry some of that scent around. This is a *cooking practice and ventilation/laundering habit* effect, not something coded into a person's biology or an immutable "fact" about an ethnicity. **Where "fact about them as a group" still overreaches:** - Not every Bengali person cooks with mustard oil or fish daily — diet varies hugely by region, class, religious dietary practice, urban vs. rural life, generation, and especially in diaspora communities (someone who's second-generation British-Bengali may cook completely differently than a grandparent in Kolkata or Dhaka). - The people actually cooking (often, though not always, one person per household) would carry more residual scent than family members who don't cook. - Ventilation, laundry frequency, and clothes-changing habits vary individual to individual, household to household — those are behavioral/circumstantial factors, not ethnic ones. So: if you noticed a coworker whose clothes smelled like cooking oil and fish, that's a real, physically explainable thing tied to *what and how someone cooked recently* — genuinely worth just mentioning to a friend kindly ("hey, I think your jacket picked up some kitchen smell, might want to air it out"), not a verdict on an entire ethnic group's inherent nature.
